{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "מסיג גבול רעהו", :translit "moseig g'vul re'ehu", :gloss "one who moves his fellow's boundary"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Metaphor: adultery as boundary violation — the adulterer displaces the husband's conjugal boundary, producing a child who does not know his father and therefore cannot honor him"}], :frame {:text "The Talmud interprets 'You shall not move your fellow's boundary' (Deuteronomy 27:17) as referring to one who seduces another man's wife, calling him 'one who moves his fellow's boundary' (Sotah 27b). The phrase 'Cursed be one who moves his fellow's boundary' (Deuteronomy 27:17) is immediately followed by 'Cursed be one who strikes his father' (Deuteronomy 27:16), which the Ba'al HaTurim reads as the direct consequence: the adulterer's unrecognised son slights his presumed father.", :sources ["Sotah 27b" "Deuteronomy 27:17" "Deuteronomy 27:16"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Metaphor: adultery as boundary violation — the adulterer displaces the husband's conjugal boundary, producing a child who does not know his father and therefore cannot honor him", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:39:44.335090378Z", :model "glm-5.2"}